Although it is [argued] that the establishment of a universal — through the impossibility of a cognition of uniform content without a single basis among different [instances] — is not valid, because there is disagreement even regarding such a perception;
even granted the validity of the positing cognition, [to say] 'it is established by that without dispute' is not valid, since it could equally be said 'it is sublated by that' —
nevertheless —

(1 <s>Sattā</s> (Existence)--) The subject under dispute, <s>dravya</s> (substance), is the <s>samavāyikāraṇa</s> (inherent cause) of things belonging to the same <s>jāti</s> (universal), because it possesses <s>samavāyikāraṇatva</s> (the nature of being an inherent cause), like clay.
Nor is the possession of touch (<s>sparśavattva</s>) a limiting condition (<s>upādhi</s>), because on your view it deviates in the case of the ultimate atoms.
But for us, when <s>sattā</s> (existence) is cognized at the time of establishing the pervasion (<s>vyāpti</s>), the proof succeeds elsewhere as well, since this objection serves as a refutation only at the time of pervasion, being a disruptor of pervasion;
because the determination of the <s>pakṣa</s> (thesis) and so forth arises only after the pervasion is established;
setting aside <s>sattā</s>, even in the absence of the <s>sādhya</s> (probandum) in the ultimate atoms, the <s>upādhi</s> (limiting condition) is still present there.
